09-03-2026 Module 5 Java Collections Framework

Iterable as root interface
    - Collection extends iterable and is the root of Collections Framework

    - List, Ordered and Duplicates allowed
    - Set, no duplicates allowed
    - Queue, Holding elements
    - Deque, Double sided, add and remove from both sides of queue

Abstract class, all abstract
Concrete Class, some implemented and not implemented

Type Safe, Generics for compile-time safety

List Interface
    - ArrayList
        - Fast at random access
        - Slow at insertion in middle
    - LinkedList
        - Pointer Items to other nodes
        - (DoublyLinkedList, has pointers at beginning and end to before and after)
        - Fast at insert and delete (update pointers)
        - Slow at random access
    - Vector (Legacy) --> ArrayList
    - Stack (Legacy) --> Deque


LinkedList
    - null indicates end of list

Set
    - No duplicate elements
    - one null allowed -> HashSet, LinkedHashSet
    - Enumset, specialized set for enums

    - General use, HashSet
    - Insertion order, LinkedHashSet
    - Sorted Order, EnumSet

    - equals(), used to compare 2 sets
    - not supported indexed access (no get or set)

HashSet
    - HashSet() -> Default capacity is 16, load factor of 0.75
    - HashMap, key and value pairs (multiple values at one key)
    - Each element is hashed
    - Adding elements as linked list
    - load factor, if capacity is greater than percentage of load factor, size of hashset increased (eg retrigger sizing on size > capacity x load factor)

TreeSet
    - Use when order matters
    - Defaults to ascending order or custom comparator
    - Comparator (O(logn)), eg TreeSet<String> set = new TreeSet<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());

