Mutability vs immutability
    - Mutable classes, can change object, not thread-safe
    - Immutable, state cannot change, any "change" creates a new object
    (eg Stringbuilder = mutable, string concat is immutable ("new" objects are made)
    - When to use which (Slide 44)


Thread safe vs thread unsafe (Slide 40.2)
    - Safe, multiple threads can access a shared resource concurrently without causing inconsistent state or data corruption
        - Use synchronized keyword on class, only one thread can execute on section at a time (locks it off)
        - Preference for parallel architecture
    - Unsafe, opposite of above


Iteration (slide 45)
    - For Loop
        - Only one that offers indexing
        - Really only for Array and List
    - For Each Loop
        - Works on arrays, collections and maps
    - Iterator
        - Safe to use for removal
    - ListIterator
        - Can go forward and backward
        - Safe to use for removal
    - Stream API


Java Collection Performance Comparison (Slide 48)
    - What collection, what operation, at what time performance
    - Tree diagram of decision (Slide 50)

    - Hash-based offer best average performance
    - Tree-based provide sorted order with log(n) operations
    - ArrayList ideal for random access
    - LinkedList for frequent insertions/deletions in the middle


